<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narbonne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Narbo Martius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narbonne is a city in the Aude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. Founded as Narbo Martius in 118 BCE, the first Roman colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, it became the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> and stood on the Via Domitia. It was later a Visigothic and, briefly, an Umayyad stronghold before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took it in 759.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narbonne.jpg" alt="Narbonne" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narbonne_Cathedrale_Saint_Just_et_Saint_Pasteur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
